  "subject": "🥕 Axios Finish Line: Food rescue",
  "date": "Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:29:22 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🧺 Plus: Kindness chain reaction | Thursday, November 12, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Nov 12, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 394 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Food gets a second chance \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n America tosses 94 billion pounds of its food each year.\n\n But many local organizations and volunteers are redirecting that food and turning it into meals for people in need. Why it matters: As nearly 50 million Americans struggle with food insecurity — lack of consistent access to nutritious food — these local groups are tackling two problems at once: food waste and hunger.\n\n 🍲 Zoom in: Cincinnati has a robust food rescue network. Local organizations recovered 17,629 tons of food in 2024 — the equivalent of 29 million meals, Axios Cincinnati's Kristen Swilley reports . \n\n Our Daily Bread, Cincinnati's largest soup kitchen, has relied entirely on rescued and donated food from restaurants and businesses throughout its 41 years of operation. Demand has doubled since 2016. \"We literally could not do what we do without rescued food,\" executive director Georgine Getty says. 🧑‍🍳 In Portland, Ore., nonprofit Urban Gleaners says it rescues nearly 1.2 million pounds of food annually and distributes it to more than 8,500 families across the region each week, Axios Portland's Meira Gebel writes . \n\n The group even hosts events inviting local chefs to make gourmet dishes out of food that would have been discarded. 🎡 In Minnesota, a group called Loaves and Fishes rescued food that didn't get sold at the Minnesota State Fair, CBS reports .\n\n The food collected from fair vendors included french fries, cheese curds and bags of milk . ⚽ In Philadelphia, 20,000 pounds of surplus food from the World Cup was delivered to families in need. Many other host cities organized similar efforts.\n\n The stadium donated nearly 14,000 pre-made meals, which included salads, wraps, cookies, chips and gluten-free options. Those meals had been made for players and staff from teams including France, Brazil, Croatia and Haiti, Axios Philadelphia's Mike D'Onofrio reports . Find your Axios Local city for news about your community. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🛒 Paying it forward \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Today's dose of kindness comes from reader Jane Remsen in Gloucester, Mass.:\n\n \"During Christmas 2024, a woman in front of me in the checkout line randomly paid for my groceries. She said, 'The world needs more random acts of kindness.'\" I have now paid for someone's groceries behind me in the line almost every month since then, and when someone asks me why, I repeat: 'The world needs more random acts of kindness!'\" \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
